`,e.jsx(i.h2,{id:"choosing-between-the-classic-package-and-the-byo-hadoop-package",children:"Choosing between the Classic Package and the BYO Hadoop Package"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:['Starting with HBase 3.0, HBase includes two binary packages. The classic package includes both the HBase and Hadoop components, while the Hadoop-less "Bring Your Own Hadoop" package omits the Hadoop components, and uses the files from an existing Hadoop installation. The classic binary package filename is named ',e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase-VERSION-bin.tar.gz"})," (e.g. ",e.jsx(i.em,{children:"hbase-3.0.0-bin.tar.gz"}),"), while the Hadoop-less package is ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase-byo-hadoop-VERSION-bin.tar.gz"})," (e.g. ",e.jsx(i.em,{children:"hbase-byo-hadoop-3.0.0-bin.tar.gz"}),")."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["If the cluster nodes already have Hadoop installed, you can use the Hadoop-less package. In this case you need to make sure that the ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"HADOOP_HOME"})," environment variable is set and points to the Hadoop installation. The easiest way to ensure this is to set it in ",e.jsx(i.em,{children:"hbase-env.sh"}),". You still need to make sure that the Hadoop configuration files are present on the HBase classpath, as described above."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h2,{id:"advantages-of-the-byo-hadoop-package",children:"Advantages of the BYO Hadoop package:"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(i.li,{children:"There is no need to replace the Hadoop libraries."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.li,{children:"It is easier to upgrade Hadoop and HBase independently (as long as compatible versions are used)."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.li,{children:"Both the package and installed size are about 100 MB smaller."}),`
