`,e.jsx(s,{type:"info",children:e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"DisabledRegionSplitPolicy"})," policy blocks manual region splitting."]})}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h2,{id:"manual-region-splitting",children:"Manual Region Splitting"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"It is possible to manually split your table, either at table creation (pre-splitting), or at a later time as an administrative action. You might choose to split your region for one or more of the following reasons. There may be other valid reasons, but the need to manually split your table might also point to problems with your schema design."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Reasons to Manually Split Your Table:"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(i.li,{children:["Your data is sorted by timeseries or another similar algorithm that sorts new data at the end of the table. This means that the Region Server holding the last region is always under load, and the other Region Servers are idle, or mostly idle. See also ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/regionserver-sizing#monotonically-increasing-row-keystimeseries-data",children:"Monotonically Increasing Row Keys/Timeseries Data"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.li,{children:["You have developed an unexpected hotspot in one region of your table. For instance, an application which tracks web searches might be inundated by a lot of searches for a celebrity in the event of news about that celebrity. See ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/performance#anti-pattern-one-hot-region",children:"perf.one.region"})," for more discussion about this particular scenario."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.li,{children:"After a big increase in the number of RegionServers in your cluster, to get the load spread out quickly."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.li,{children:"Before a bulk-load which is likely to cause unusual and uneven load across regions."}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["See ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/configuration/important#managed-splitting",children:"Managed Splitting"})," for a discussion about the dangers and possible benefits of managing splitting completely manually."]}),`
`,e.jsx(s,{type:"info",children:e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"DisabledRegionSplitPolicy"})," policy blocks manual region splitting."]})}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h3,{id:"determining-split-points",children:"Determining Split Points"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"The goal of splitting your table manually is to improve the chances of balancing the load across the cluster in situations where good rowkey design alone won't get you there. Keeping that in mind, the way you split your regions is very dependent upon the characteristics of your data. It may be that you already know the best way to split your table. If not, the way you split your table depends on what your keys are like."}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Alphanumeric Rowkeys"}),e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"If your rowkeys start with a letter or number, you can split your table at letter or number boundaries. For instance, the following command creates a table with regions that split at each vowel, so the first region has A-D, the second region has E-H, the third region has I-N, the fourth region has O-V, and the fifth region has U-Z."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Using a Custom Algorithm"}),e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"The RegionSplitter tool is provided with HBase, and uses a ",e.jsx(i.em,{children:"SplitAlgorithm"})," to determine split points for you. As parameters, you give it the algorithm, desired number of regions, and column families. It includes three split algorithms. The first is the ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"https://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/util/RegionSplitter.HexStringSplit.html",children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"HexStringSplit"})})," algorithm, which assumes the row keys are hexadecimal strings. The second is the ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"https://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/util/RegionSplitter.DecimalStringSplit.html",children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"DecimalStringSplit"})})," algorithm, which assumes the row keys are decimal strings in the range 00000000 to 99999999. The third, ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"https://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/util/RegionSplitter.UniformSplit.html",children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"UniformSplit"})}),", assumes the row keys are random byte arrays. You will probably need to develop your own ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"https://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/util/RegionSplitter.SplitAlgorithm.html",children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"SplitAlgorithm"})}),", using the provided ones as models."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h2,{id:"online-region-merges",children:"Online Region Merges"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["Both Master and RegionServer participate in the event of online region merges. Client sends merge RPC to the master, then the master moves the regions together to the RegionServer where the more heavily loaded region resided. Finally the master sends the merge request to this RegionServer which then runs the merge. Similar to process of region splitting, region merges run as a local transaction on the RegionServer. It offlines the regions and then merges two regions on the file system, atomically delete merging regions from ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase:meta"})," and adds the merged region to ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase:meta"}),", opens the merged region on the RegionServer and reports the merge to the Master."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"An example of region merges in the HBase shell"}),`
