`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Region and stripe sizing"}),e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"You can configure your stripe sizing based upon your region sizing. By default, your new regions will start with one stripe. On the next compaction after the stripe has grown too large (16 x MemStore flushes size), it is split into two stripes. Stripe splitting continues as the region grows, until the region is large enough to split."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"You can improve this pattern for your own data. A good rule is to aim for a stripe size of at least 1 GB, and about 8-12 stripes for uniform row keys. For example, if your regions are 30 GB, 12 x 2.5 GB stripes might be a good starting point."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Stripe Sizing Settings"})}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.table,{children:[e.jsx(i.thead,{children:e.jsxs(i.tr,{children:[e.jsx(i.th,{children:"Setting"}),e.jsx(i.th,{children:"Notes"})]})}),e.jsxs(i.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(i.tr,{children:[e.jsx(i.td,{children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.initialStripeCount"})}),e.jsxs(i.td,{children:["The number of stripes to create when stripe compaction is enabled. You can use it as follows:",e.jsx(i.br,{}),"- For relatively uniform row keys, if you know the approximate target number of stripes from the above, you can avoid some splitting overhead by starting with several stripes (2, 5, 10...). If the early data is not representative of overall row key distribution, this will not be as efficient.",e.jsx(i.br,{}),"- For existing tables with a large amount of data, this setting will effectively pre-split your stripes.",e.jsx(i.br,{}),"- For keys such as hash-prefixed sequential keys, with more than one hash prefix per region, pre-splitting may make sense."]})]}),e.jsxs(i.tr,{children:[e.jsx(i.td,{children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.sizeToSplit"})}),e.jsxs(i.td,{children:["The maximum size a stripe grows before splitting. Use this in conjunction with ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.splitPartCount"})," to control the target stripe size (",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"sizeToSplit = splitPartsCount * target stripe size"}),"), according to the above sizing considerations."]})]}),e.jsxs(i.tr,{children:[e.jsx(i.td,{children:e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.splitPartCount"})}),e.jsx(i.td,{children:"The number of new stripes to create when splitting a stripe. The default is 2, which is appropriate for most cases. For non-uniform row keys, you can experiment with increasing the number to 3 or 4, to isolate the arriving updates into narrower slice of the region without additional splits being required."})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"MemStore Size Settings"}),e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"By default, the flush creates several files from one MemStore, according to existing stripe boundaries and row keys to flush. This approach minimizes write amplification, but can be undesirable if the MemStore is small and there are many stripes, because the files will be too small.",e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"In this type of situation, you can set ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.compaction.flushToL0"})," to ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"true"}),". This will cause a MemStore flush to create a single file instead. When at least ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.compaction.minFilesL0"})," such files (by default, 4) accumulate, they will be compacted into striped files."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:[e.jsx(i.strong,{children:"Normal Compaction Configuration and Stripe Compaction"}),e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"All the settings that apply to normal compactions (see ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm",children:"Parameters Used by Compaction Algorithm"}),") apply to stripe compactions. The exceptions are the minimum and maximum number of files, which are set to higher values by default because the files in stripes are smaller. To control these for stripe compactions, use ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.compaction.minFiles"})," and ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.store.stripe.compaction.maxFiles"}),", rather than ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.hstore.compaction.min"})," and ",e.jsx(i.code,{children:"hbase.hstore.compaction.max"}),"."]}),`
