`,e.jsxs(e.Fragment,{children:[e.jsx(i.h2,{id:"hbase-backup",children:"HBase Backup"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"There are two broad strategies for performing HBase backups: backing up with a full cluster shutdown, and backing up on a live cluster. Each approach has pros and cons."}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["For additional information, see ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"https://web.archive.org/web/20160110232448/http://blog.sematext.com/2011/03/11/hbase-backup-options/",children:"HBase Backup Options"})," over on the Sematext Blog."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h3,{id:"full-shutdown-backup",children:"Full Shutdown Backup"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"Some environments can tolerate a periodic full shutdown of their HBase cluster, for example if it is being used a back-end analytic capacity and not serving front-end web-pages. The benefits are that the NameNode/Master are RegionServers are down, so there is no chance of missing any in-flight changes to either StoreFiles or metadata. The obvious con is that the cluster is down. The steps include:"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h4,{id:"operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stop-hbase",children:"Stop HBase"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h4,{id:"distcp",children:"Distcp"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["Distcp could be used to either copy the contents of the HBase directory in HDFS to either the same cluster in another directory, or to a different cluster.",e.jsx(i.br,{}),`
`,"Note: Distcp works in this situation because the cluster is down and there are no in-flight edits to files. Distcp-ing of files in the HBase directory is not generally recommended on a live cluster."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h4,{id:"restore-if-needed",children:"Restore (if needed)"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"The backup of the hbase directory from HDFS is copied onto the 'real' hbase directory via distcp. The act of copying these files creates new HDFS metadata, which is why a restore of the NameNode edits from the time of the HBase backup isn't required for this kind of restore, because it's a restore (via distcp) of a specific HDFS directory (i.e., the HBase part) not the entire HDFS file-system."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h3,{id:"live-cluster-backup---replication",children:"Live Cluster Backup - Replication"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["This approach assumes that there is a second cluster. See the HBase page on ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication",children:"replication"})," for more information."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h3,{id:"live-cluster-backup---copytable",children:"Live Cluster Backup - CopyTable"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable",children:"copytable"})," utility could either be used to copy data from one table to another on the same cluster, or to copy data to another table on another cluster."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"Since the cluster is up, there is a risk that edits could be missed in the copy process."}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h3,{id:"live-cluster-backup---export",children:"Live Cluster Backup - Export"}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["The ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/operational-management/tools#export",children:"export"})," approach dumps the content of a table to HDFS on the same cluster. To restore the data, the ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/operational-management/tools#import",children:"import"})," utility would be used."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(i.p,{children:["Since the cluster is up, there is a risk that edits could be missed in the export process. If you want to know more about HBase back-up and restore see the page on ",e.jsx(i.a,{href:"/docs/backup-restore",children:"Backup and Restore"}),"."]}),`
`,e.jsx(i.h2,{id:"hbase-snapshots",children:"HBase Snapshots"}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:'HBase Snapshots allow you to take a copy of a table (both contents and metadata)with a very small performance impact. A Snapshot is an immutable collection of table metadata and a list of HFiles that comprised the table at the time the Snapshot was taken. A "clone" of a snapshot creates a new table from that snapshot, and a "restore" of a snapshot returns the contents of a table to what it was when the snapshot was created. The "clone" and "restore" operations do not require any data to be copied, as the underlying HFiles (the files which contain the data for an HBase table) are not modified with either action. Simiarly, exporting a snapshot to another cluster has little impact on RegionServers of the local cluster.'}),`
`,e.jsx(i.p,{children:"Prior to version 0.94.6, the only way to backup or to clone a table is to use CopyTable/ExportTable, or to copy all the hfiles in HDFS after disabling the table. The disadvantages of these methods are that you can degrade region server performance (Copy/Export Table) or you need to disable the table, that means no reads or writes; and this is usually unacceptable."}),`
